Let's take a closer look at the `lammps` module. LAMMPS is a widely used
materials modelling package. Different versions will potentially have different capabilities.
In this case, there
are six different verions.
How do we load each copy and which copy is the default?

In this case, ``lammps/17Feb2023 has a `(D)` next to it. This indicates that it
is the default - if we type `module load lammps`, this is the copy that will be
loaded.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load lammps
{{ site.remote.prompt }} lmp -h
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Large-scale Atomic/Molecular Massively Parallel Simulator - 23 Jun 2022 - Update 3

..trimmed...
```
{: .output}

So how do we load the non-default copy of a software package? In this case, the
only change we need to make is be more specific about the module we are
loading.
To load a non-default module, we need to make add the version number after the
`/` in our `module load` command

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load lammps/29Aug2024
{{ site.remote.prompt }} lmp -h
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Large-scale Atomic/Molecular Massively Parallel Simulator - 29 Aug 2024 - Update 1

..trimmed...
```
{: .output}

